// lib/sales/calls/schema.pending.prisma
//
// NOT READ BY PRISMA. Prisma reads prisma/schema.prisma and nothing else; this
// is a parking space, exactly like lib/sales/playbook/schema.pending.prisma,
// and lib/sales/calls/store.js points at it in the message it shows while the
// tables are absent.
//
// The owner holds the schema. Nothing in this session ran `prisma db push`.
//
// To land it: paste the models below into prisma/schema.prisma beside the
// other sales models, add the four one-line relation backs listed at the
// bottom, then:
//
//     npx prisma validate && npx prisma db push && npx prisma generate
//
// Nothing else has to change. store.js probes the generated client for the
// delegates, so the disposition control, the cap enforcement and the team
// screens turn themselves on the moment the models exist — and stay honestly
// absent until then.
//
// ── One thing to do in the same commit ────────────────────────────────────
//
// scripts/check-sales-calling-window.mjs carries a tripwire that ASSERTS
// `SalesCallAttempt` is not in prisma/schema.prisma. It is not stale and it is
// not in the way: its message is "if this fails, wire the cap up". Adding the
// model flips it, and the assertion beside it — that the queue route passes
// `attemptsLast24h` — is what replaces it. Both are already written; see that
// file's "the cap" section.

/// One press of the call button.
///
/// ── Written at the DIAL, not at the outcome ──────────────────────────────
///
/// Oklahoma and Florida cap CALLS to the same business on the same subject at
/// three per 24 hours — not conversations. A rep who dials five times and
/// fills one outcome in has made five calls, so the row exists before there is
/// anything to say about it and `disposition` starts null. An attempt with no
/// disposition is a real, visible state, not a gap.
///
/// ── Two ways a call can be placed, and only one of them is measurable ────
///
/// `dialChannel` says which. A `handset` dial is a `tel:` handoff: the
/// operating system takes the call and nothing reports back, so every provider
/// column below stays null forever on those rows. A `browser` dial is bridged
/// through Twilio, and the provider reports ring, answer, hangup and price.
///
/// The columns are nullable rather than absent BECAUSE the two coexist. What
/// must never happen is a report that averages them together and calls the
/// result talk time — a handset row is not a zero-second call, it is a call
/// nobody timed. lib/sales/calls/reporting.js counts the measured rows and
/// prints the denominator beside every duration for exactly that reason.
model SalesCallAttempt {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// Who dialled. Restrict, not Cascade: a rep leaving must not delete the
  /// evidence that a business was called. Same reasoning SalesSuppression
  /// gives for keeping loose pointers instead of cascading foreign keys.
  salesRepId String
  salesRep   SalesRep @relation(fields: [salesRepId], references: [id])

  /// The prospect the rep was looking at, when there was one. Nullable and
  /// SetNull: a rep ringing a lead they typed in themselves has no Prospect,
  /// and deleting one must not delete the call record.
  prospectId String?
  prospect   Prospect? @relation(fields: [prospectId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  /// The rep's own lead, when there is one.
  leadId String?
  lead   SalesLead? @relation(fields: [leadId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  /// The number as dialled, E.164, normalised on write.
  ///
  /// THE CAP IS COUNTED ON THIS COLUMN, not on prospectId. Prospect dedupe
  /// FLAGS duplicates rather than merging them — `possibleDuplicateOfId`,
  /// "merging destroys provenance" — so one business can hold two rows, and
  /// counting per row would turn a three-call cap into a six-call one by
  /// accident.
  toE164 String

  /// The number presented to them. Null on a handset dial, because nothing in
  /// FieldQuo knows what a rep's own phone presents. Not defaulted to the sales
  /// line: that would record a caller ID that was never shown.
  fromE164 String?

  /// "out" | "in". Inbound rows are written by the sales-line handler when a
  /// contractor rings back, so "how many times have we spoken" is one query.
  direction String @default("out")

  /// "browser" | "handset". Which door the call went through, and therefore
  /// whether any of the provider columns below can ever be filled. Defaulted
  /// to handset so a row written by an older path is not silently claimed as
  /// measured.
  dialChannel String @default("handset")

  dialledAt DateTime @default(now())

  // ── What the carrier reported. Browser dials only ─────────────────────
  //
  // Every one of these is nullable and stays null on a handset row. None is
  // ever defaulted to zero: "not measured" and "zero seconds" are different
  // facts and collapsing them is what turns an honest gap into a false chart.

  /// Twilio's CallSid for the PSTN leg. @unique so the three status callbacks
  /// for one call (initiated, answered, completed) land on one row and a
  /// provider retry cannot duplicate it — the same reason
  /// PlatformVoiceCall.providerCallId is unique.
  providerCallSid String? @unique
  /// Twilio's own final status: completed, busy, no-answer, failed, canceled.
  /// Kept verbatim and NOT mapped onto a disposition: what the network says
  /// and what the rep says are two different statements about one call, and
  /// merging them loses the case where they disagree.
  providerStatus  String?
  /// When it started ringing, when it was answered, when it ended.
  ringingAt   DateTime?
  answeredAt  DateTime?
  endedAt     DateTime?
  /// Billed seconds, from the provider. This IS talk time, on these rows only.
  talkSeconds Int?
  /// Seconds the rep had the prospect on hold, summed from hold events.
  holdSeconds Int?
  /// FieldQuo's own cost for this call, both legs, from the provider's figure.
  /// Null when the provider gave none — never a hand-typed per-minute
  /// estimate, for the reason VoiceCall.providerCostCents gives.
  providerCostCents Decimal? @db.Decimal(12, 4)

  // ── The gate's answer, frozen at the moment of the dial ────────────────
  //
  // "Was this call lawful when it was made" has to be answerable a year later,
  // after CALLING_JURISDICTIONS has been edited and a state has moved from
  // unverified to verified. Recomputing it then would answer a different
  // question. So the decision travels with the row.

  /// The CALLING_JURISDICTIONS key that was applied — "US-OK", "CA", and so on.
  /// Null only for a lead with no jurisdiction, which cannot produce an
  /// allowed decision and therefore cannot produce a row today.
  jurisdictionCode String?
  /// Always "allowed" — dialHref() cannot return a target from anything else.
  /// Stored rather than assumed so the invariant is auditable in the data
  /// instead of only in the code that wrote it.
  decisionAtDial   String  @default("allowed")
  /// The window as it read on screen, e.g. "08:00–20:00".
  windowText       String?
  /// The zone the window was evaluated in, and whether a human stated it or it
  /// was derived from the subdivision. The two are not equally good and the
  /// difference must survive.
  prospectTimeZone String?
  timeZoneSource   String?

  // ── What the rep says happened ────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Null until they say. A DISPOSITIONS key from lib/sales/calls/dispositions.js
  // — a closed vocabulary, checked in code, deliberately not a superadmin table
  // because each value carries a claim transition with it.

  disposition     String?
  dispositionAt   DateTime?
  dispositionNote String?

  /// Set only by the `callback` disposition. The claim's lease is extended to
  /// cover it, which is why dispositions.js bounds how far out it may be.
  callbackAt DateTime?

  // ── What was said, so an experiment can be read afterwards ────────────
  //
  // SalesPlaybookExperiment and SalesPlaybookAssignment already store which
  // arm a prospect is in, BEFORE the call, per spec §38 — and there has been
  // nothing to measure them against. These four columns are that missing half.
  // Copied at dial time rather than joined at read time: editing a playbook
  // must not retroactively change what a rep is recorded as having read.

  playbookKey     String?
  playbookVersion String?
  experimentKey   String?
  variantKey      String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// The cap query, and the only index that has to be fast: every dial runs it.
  @@index([toE164, dialledAt])
  @@index([salesRepId, dialledAt])
  @@index([prospectId, dialledAt])
  @@index([disposition])
}

// ── On `model PlatformSmsNumber` ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two additions, and one honest admission.
//
// The sales floor needs numbers to CALL from — local ones, because a
// contractor in Tulsa answers a 918 number and lets an unknown one ring out.
// The telephony audit is explicit that these must not live in
// `VoicePhoneNumber`: its `companyId` is a required FK and `heldNumber()`
// enforces one per company, so a pool is structurally the thing that code
// treats as a bug, and putting them there would make the rent cron bill a
// non-company and report a false billing leak per number.
//
// `PlatformSmsNumber` is the only tenant-free number model there is, so a
// voice number goes here with `purpose: "sales_voice"`. The admission: the
// model's NAME has outgrown it — it is a platform number table now, not an SMS
// one. Renaming it is a schema decision the owner owns, and inventing a second
// tenant-free number table to avoid the rename would be the parallel system
// AGENTS.md warns about. So: same table, new purpose, and this note so nobody
// concludes from the name that voice numbers must live somewhere else.
//
//     /// The voice webhook, for a "sales_voice" number. Null for every other
//     /// purpose — an SMS number with a voice URL would answer calls nobody
//     /// meant it to answer.
//     voiceUrl String?
//
// `purpose` gains one documented value, "sales_voice", beside system,
// shared_test and sales. No schema change — it is already a plain String.

/// What a rep is doing right now, and the log of what they were doing.
///
/// ── One model, not two ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// A contact centre usually keeps a live agent-status row and a separate
/// activity log, because a predictive dialler has to read the live one
/// thousands of times a second. FieldQuo has no dialler reading it: the only
/// consumer is a person looking at a screen. So this is the LOG, and "what are
/// they doing right now" is its most recent row — one table that cannot
/// disagree with itself, rather than two that can. The same argument
/// lib/voice/credits.js makes for summing a balance instead of storing one.
///
/// ── Nothing infers a state ────────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// Every row is written because a rep pressed something, or because they
/// dialled, or because their session ended. Nothing writes "available" because
/// a page loaded, and nothing writes "offline" on a guess about a browser that
/// stopped polling — a stale row says when it was written and the screen says
/// how old it is. Inventing presence is AGENTS.md failure class 5 pointed at a
/// person's timesheet.
model SalesRepActivity {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  salesRepId String
  salesRep   SalesRep @relation(fields: [salesRepId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// A REP_STATES key from lib/sales/calls/agentState.js. Closed vocabulary.
  state String

  /// Why, for `paused` only — a PAUSE_REASONS key. Null for every other state,
  /// and refused as a value on any other state, so "paused" without a reason
  /// cannot exist and "available, reason: lunch" cannot either.
  pauseReason String?

  /// The attempt that caused it, for `on_call` and `after_call`. Loose pointer
  /// on purpose, like SalesSuppression's: the activity log outliving an
  /// attempt row is an acceptable failure, the log vanishing is not.
  callAttemptId String?

  startedAt DateTime @default(now())
  /// Written when the NEXT row is opened, so a period has a real length rather
  /// than one computed from the gap to whatever row happened to sort next.
  /// Null on the current row, which is how "right now" is found.
  endedAt   DateTime?

  /// Last time the rep's browser said it was still there. Beaten every 60s by
  /// the portal shell.
  ///
  /// This exists so the supervisor board can tell "available" from "said they
  /// were available before the laptop lid closed". Without it an open row is
  /// true forever and the board confidently reports a rep who went home. It is
  /// NOT used to close the row or to infer a state — nothing writes "offline"
  /// on a guess about a browser that stopped talking. It only ages the claim,
  /// and livePresence() turns that age into a third answer beside the state.
  heartbeatAt DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// The live lookup: one rep's open row, and a team's open rows.
  @@index([salesRepId, startedAt])
  @@index([endedAt, startedAt])
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Columns to ADD to models that already exist
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ── On `model SalesRep` ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The reporting line lib/sales/notes/visibility.js records the absence of, in
// as many words: "There is no reporting line on SalesRep... a manager tier here
// would mean inventing an org chart." This is that org chart, in one column,
// shaped after Worker.manager @relation("WorkerReports") which leave approval
// already uses — the same shape rather than a new one, so nobody has to learn
// a second way FieldQuo expresses "reports to".
//
// SetNull, not Cascade: a team lead leaving must not delete their reports.
// Nothing in the database can stop A reporting to B reporting to A; the cycle
// guard is in lib/sales/team.js and is executed by the check script.
//
//     managerId String?
//     manager   SalesRep?  @relation("SalesRepReports", fields: [managerId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
//     reports   SalesRep[] @relation("SalesRepReports")
//
//     @@index([managerId])
//
// and the two relation backs for the models above:
//
//     callAttempts SalesCallAttempt[]
//     activity     SalesRepActivity[]

// ── On `model Prospect` ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//     callAttempts SalesCallAttempt[]

// ── On `model SalesLead` ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//     callAttempts SalesCallAttempt[]

// ── On `model PlatformVoiceCall` ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// An inbound call to FieldQuo's own sales line currently lands with fromE164
// and nothing else — the schema's own comment calls it "the only contact
// detail a sales call leaves behind". These columns are the match, and what
// they are allowed to mean is the important part:
//
//   A caller ID is FORGEABLE. Unlike SalesThread.replyToken, which is a secret
//   we issued and is therefore proof, a matched number is a HINT. It may
//   annotate a call and route it to the rep who already holds the claim. It
//   may never authenticate anybody, lift a suppression, move a claim, or
//   credit an attribution. lib/sales/calls/inboundMatch.js enforces that and
//   its header argues it.
//
//     prospectId  String?
//     prospect    Prospect?  @relation(fields: [prospectId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
//     salesLeadId String?
//     salesLead   SalesLead? @relation(fields: [salesLeadId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
//     /// How the match was made — "phone_e164" today. Null when nothing matched,
//     /// which is the common case and is not a failure.
//     matchedBy   String?
//     matchedAt   DateTime?
//
//     @@index([fromE164])
//
// plus the relation backs, on Prospect and SalesLead respectively:
//
//     inboundCalls PlatformVoiceCall[]
